 The finite-element section distinguishes cellwise-constant control discretization from variational control discretization. The $O(h)$ result assumes $H^1$ control regularity, a uniform $H^1$ bound for the projected adjoint, and a complete first-Strang gradient-residual ledger. The conditional $O(h^2)$ result requires actual primal and adjoint $H^2$ regularity, matching dual approximation, uniform state/adjoint $L^2$ errors, and every form, load, geometry, and quadrature defect at the stated order. It makes no rate claim on arbitrary Lipschitz or reentrant domains.
 
-The finite-loop projected-gradient method optimizes the exact continuous PDE objective over a cellwise-constant control box while using finite-element state/adjoint solves with explicit Strang residual bounds. Its certified status bounds the exact projected-gradient residual; a separate theorem converts that residual to distance from the restricted optimum. The exhaustive active-set algorithm is an exact-arithmetic finite-dimensional reference. The SQP branch reports only a certified active linear-system residual unless feasibility, complementarity, conditioning, and the required local hypotheses are separately verified. The parabolic extension is a separate energy-space result and does not inherit an elliptic finite-element rate.
+The finite-loop projected-gradient method optimizes the exact continuous PDE objective over a cellwise-constant control box while using finite-element state/adjoint solves with explicit Strang residual bounds. Its certified status bounds the exact projected-gradient residual; a separate theorem converts that residual to distance from the restricted optimum. The exhaustive active-set algorithm is an exact-arithmetic finite-dimensional reference. The SQP branch reports only a certified active linear-system residual unless feasibility, complementarity, conditioning, and the required local hypotheses are separately verified. The parabolic extension assumes a real evolution triple with separable energy space $V$ and is a separate energy-space result and does not inherit an elliptic finite-element rate.
